// @run
// @log 6
// @log x
// @hint redundant type annotation: `s` is inferred as `i32` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `t` is inferred as `i32` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `u` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// @hint redundant type annotation: `v` is inferred as `string` — remove it to lean on inference
// `??` OVER AN OPERAND THE PIN PROVES NON-NULLABLE (silent-class-inventory D512).
//
// The emitter folds `a ?? b` to `a` when the left operand provably admits no null, and that
// fold is in `dispatchRewrite` — which runs BEFORE monomorphization, so inside a generic body
// it asks `nodeTyExcludesNull` of an operand still typed `T`. Every "don't know" answers FALSE
// there (deliberately: a wrong TRUE is a rep mismatch), so the `??` node survived and reached
// `emitProgram: `??` over this nullable value is not supported yet` — while the DIRECT
// spelling of the same program ran. A capability lost through a type parameter, across 15 of
// the 16 operand types a hole can be pinned to.
//
// TWO PINS OF ONE GENERIC, and the second is not decoration: the fold returns the left
// operand's own node, so an i32 instance and a `string` instance must each keep their own rep.
// A one-pin file would pass with the fold hard-wired to the i32 path.
//
// The annotations the hints name are LOAD-BEARING — they ARE the pins — and are declared
// rather than deleted.
function coal<T>(a: T, b: T): T { return a ?? b }
const s: i32 = 6
const t: i32 = 3
print(coal(s, t))
const u: string = "x"
const v: string = "y"
print(coal(u, v))
